{
  "name": "pulse_wave",
  "about": "Five on/off cycles of two seconds each, the shape a pulse-wave botnet produces when it rotates its vector faster than a one-second loop can follow. Every burst carries real invalid packets and one unified-list entry taking hits, so the demand genuinely swings between rungs. The assertion is that the rung actually in force does not swing with it: the transition count is bounded.",
  "magnitudes": "PARAMETERS. The on phase is set at 800 000 over-budget packets per second with 60 000 amplified-DNS signature matches and 40 000 reverse-path failures per second, and the entry at 203.0.113.7 taking 30 000 hits per second. The two-second period is the parameter that matters: it is chosen shorter than the descent this engine allows (fall_ticks defaults to 5 slow ticks), which is exactly the condition under which a naive threshold oscillates.",
  "period_ms": 100,
  "entry_v4": [203, 0, 113, 7],
